course: money
lesson: 29
title: Jobs and what people earn
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most grown-ups get their money by working: they swap their time and skills for pay, either by the hour or as a yearly salary.
explain: Picture Ella's dad, a bus driver on about £15 an hour: a full week of 40 hours earns 40 × £15 = £600 before tax, and that pays for the family's rent, food and bills.
explain: Pay varies hugely: a carer may get about £11 an hour, a plumber may charge £60 an hour, a teacher was reported to earn about £30,000 to £40,000 a year in England, and a top footballer can earn a million or more.
explain: A law called the minimum wage sets the least a boss may pay per hour, so nobody is paid next to nothing for a full day's work.
explain: Fame jobs look easy on screen, but most YouTubers earn almost nothing, while ordinary jobs like driving, caring and building keep the country running.
fact: A salary is yearly pay; an hourly rate is pay for each hour worked; a career is the work someone does over their life.
fact: It was reported that teachers in England earn about £30,000 to £40,000 a year, while a top-league footballer can earn £1 million a year or more.
fact: Full-time work is about 35 to 40 hours a week; at £15 an hour, 40 hours earns 40 × £15 = £600.
fact: A skilled trade is a job needing special training with your hands, like plumbing; at £60 an hour, 10 hours of jobs bring in £600 in a day before costs.
fact: A care worker helps people who cannot manage alone; at £11 an hour, 8 hours earns £88, and people argue about whether such important jobs should pay more.
fact: The minimum wage is the lowest pay per hour allowed by law; at roughly £12 an hour, as reported, 8 hours earns 8 × £12 = £96.
fact: YouTubers earn from ads on their videos; 1,000 views might earn about £1, so most earn little or nothing.
fact: Some high-paying jobs need long hours: £60,000 a year is £5,000 a month, because £60,000 ÷ 12 = £5,000, but it can mean late evenings at work.
fact: Work-life balance means having time both for work and for family.
fact: Jobs change over time; coal miners once worked 6 days of 10 hours, which is 60 hours a week, and many mines have since closed.
fact: People with more responsibility, like a head teacher, usually earn more; a head teacher might earn about £70,000, twice a teacher's £35,000.
fact: Teenagers can have small part-time jobs from a certain age, often 13 or more, with rules; 6 hours at £8 is £48 for a Saturday.
exercise: What is a salary? | Pay for a whole year of work | Pay for one single hour of work | Money you get back from a shop | A present from your boss at Christmas
exercise: What was reported about teachers' pay in England? | About £30,000 to £40,000 a year | About £1 million a year | About £3,000 a year | About £300,000 a year
exercise: Ella's dad earns £15 an hour and works 40 hours. How much does he earn that week? | £600 | £55 | £400 | £640
exercise: About how many hours a week is full-time work? | 35 to 40 hours | 5 to 10 hours | 80 to 90 hours | 1 to 2 hours
exercise: A plumber charges £60 an hour and works 10 hours of jobs in a day. How much comes in before costs? | £600 | £70 | £60 | £6,000
exercise: Why can skilled trades like plumbing pay well? | People really need them, and not everyone can do them | Plumbers are paid by the government to be rich | Every plumber owns a big company | Pipes cost so little that plumbers keep it all
exercise: Mia's mum is a carer on £11 an hour. How much does she earn in 8 hours? | £88 | £19 | £81 | £96
exercise: What is the minimum wage? | The lowest pay per hour a boss may legally pay | The most a boss is allowed to pay anyone each hour | The pay children get for doing chores around the house | A bonus paid to the hardest worker at the end of the year
exercise: At roughly £12 an hour, how much is 8 hours on the minimum wage? | £96 | £20 | £86 | £120
exercise: A video gets 1,000 views and earns about £1 from ads. What would 1,000,000 views earn? | About £1,000 | About £1 | About £100 | About £1,000,000
exercise: What is true about most YouTubers? | They earn little or nothing | They all earn millions from ads | They are paid by schools to make videos | They earn a fixed salary from YouTube
exercise: Nina's mum earns £60,000 a year. How much is that a month? | £5,000 | £6,000 | £500 | £50,000
exercise: Tom's grandad mined 6 days a week, 10 hours a day. How many hours a week is that? | 60 | 16 | 50 | 70
exercise: Why does a head teacher usually earn more than the teachers? | She has more responsibility, running the whole school | She teaches more lessons every week than any other teacher | Head teachers are paid directly by the parents of the pupils | She has been working at the school for longer than anyone
exercise: A teacher earns £35,000 and the head about twice that. What does the head earn? | £70,000 | £37,000 | £35,000 | £105,000
exercise: Kai's cousin works 6 hours at £8 an hour on Saturday. How much is the first pay packet? | £48 | £14 | £42 | £68
test: The class shouts "YouTuber!" and Leo says, "I'll make vids and get rich, easy." His sister says, "Most get like £0, mate." Who's right? | His sister: most earn little or nothing, only a few get rich | Leo: anyone who posts videos gets rich quickly | Leo: YouTube pays everyone a salary | Neither: YouTubers are paid by the government
test: Sami says, "My uncle's a plumber and he's minted!" His mate goes, "Nah, plumbers ain't rich." ("Minted" means rich.) What did the teacher say? | Some do very well; skilled trades can pay well | Plumbers always earn the least of any job in the country | Only footballers and pop stars can ever be minted | Plumbing is unpaid work, so the uncle can't be rich
test: A grandad from Durham says, "Hard graft, pet, sixty hours down the pit." ("Hard graft" means very hard work; "the pit" is the coal mine.) If he worked 6 days, how many hours a day? | 10 | 6 | 54 | 12
test: Your mate goes, "Carers get paid loads cos it's such an important job." What's actually true? | Many carers are low-paid, and people argue about whether they should get more | Carers are the best-paid workers in the country | Carers are paid by the hour more than doctors | Carers are volunteers and are never paid at all
test: What would happen if there were no minimum wage at all? | Some bosses could pay workers very little per hour | Everybody would be paid exactly the same | Everybody's pay would double overnight | Nobody would be allowed to work any more
test: Nina's mum says, "Good money, but I never see you." What is she talking about? | Work-life balance: high pay can mean less family time | Her job pays badly but she has loads of free time | She is working as an invisible magician | She is saying she never wants to see Nina again
test: In a cartoon, a bus driver earns £1 a year and a TV star earns £1 a year too. What's wrong? | Real pay varies, and a bus driver earns far more than £1 a year | Nothing, everyone really earns £1 a year | Bus drivers really earn more than TV stars | Pay is the same for every job by law
test: A Gen Alpha cousin says, "My cousin got his first pay packet, he's buzzing, it's giving rich." ("Buzzing" means very happy; "it's giving" means it looks like.) He worked 6 hours at £8 — no, £9 — an hour. How much did he get? | £54 | £48 | £15 | £69
test: Ella's dad earns £15 an hour. He works 35 hours one week. How much does he earn? | £525 | £600 | £50 | £535
test: Ruby goes, "Head teacher earns more cos she's the oldest, innit." What's a better reason? | She has more responsibility, running the whole school | She shouts the loudest in assemblies | Older people always earn the most, whatever job they do | She owns the school building
test: Your nan says, "Back in my day, the mines employed half the town." What's true about jobs over time? | Jobs change; many mines closed and new kinds of work appeared | Jobs never change, so the mines are all still open | Every miner became a YouTuber | Coal mining is now the biggest job in Britain
exam: It is given that an employee is remunerated at £15 per hour and works forty hours in a week. Determine the gross weekly remuneration. | £600 | £55 | £400 | £640
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the minimum wage? | The lowest hourly rate of pay which an employer may lawfully offer | The greatest hourly rate of pay which an employer may lawfully offer | A payment made to persons engaged in domestic chores | An award conferred upon the most diligent employee
exam: Ascertain the earnings of a care worker remunerated at £11 per hour in respect of a period of eight hours. | £88 | £19 | £81 | £96
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the earnings of persons who publish videos online. | The majority receive little or no income from such activity | All receive a salary from the platform on which they publish | All receive an income in excess of one million pounds annually | Their income is determined each year by the government of the day
